1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
This is silver.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:08,240
You probably think it's gold's cheaper little sibling, the metal for spoons, anklets and second place medals.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:14,860
But for most of human history, silver, not gold, was the money that ordinary people actually used.

4
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:20,000
A lucky strike of silver built the warships that saved ancient Athens.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:27,800
A mountain of it in South America built a city as big as London, and killed thousands of the people who mined it.

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:35,840
And two Texas brothers tried to buy up a huge share of the world's silver, and watched it all collapse in a single day.

7
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:42,020
Silver conducts electricity better than any other metal on Earth, and it reflects light better than any other metal too.

8
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:46,680
That's why it's inside your phone, your car and almost every solar panel.

9
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:54,420
And remember this, because unlike gold, a huge share of the silver we dig up is used once, and then it's gone for good.

10
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:59,320
It wasn't silver's beauty that made it so powerful, and it wasn't its rarity.

11
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:06,760
It was usefulness, first as money in every market, and now as a metal the modern world can't run without.

12
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:13,460
Now, let's take a closer look at the four and a half billion year story of the metal that paid the world.

13
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:17,060
Silver is older than the Earth itself.

14
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:30,160
Scientists believe it was forged inside dying stars and in some of the most violent explosions in the universe, from giant stars blowing apart to dead neutron stars smashing together.

15
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:36,260
Some astronomers even think silver and gold were mostly made in different kinds of cosmic events.

16
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,740
About four and a half billion years ago, those atoms became part of the new Earth.

17
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:53,440
Today, silver is nearly twenty times more common than gold in the Earth's crust, but it's still rare, about one gram in thirteen tonnes of rock.

18
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:01,440
And unlike gold, silver rarely sits around in pure nuggets, which is why humans found it so much later.

19
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:06,660
Most of the world's silver is hidden inside other ores, especially lead.

20
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:14,360
More than five thousand years ago, in what's now Turkey, Syria and Iran, metalworkers figured out how to get it out.

21
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:22,460
They melted the ore and blew air over it, until the lead burned away and a tiny bead of shining silver was left behind.

22
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:32,840
The process is called cupellation, and archaeologists have found its waste, a lead residue called litharge, at sites more than five thousand years old.

23
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,780
It was dirty, toxic and slow work.

24
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:41,280
But it meant that, for the first time, people could make silver wherever they could find the right rock.

25
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:49,140
In ancient Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, silver became something new, money.

26
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:55,000
There were no coins yet, so people weighed out pieces of silver, using a unit called the shekel.

27
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:01,600
Rings, coils and scraps of silver were cut and weighed to pay for grain, land and debts.

28
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:11,000
Around 1750 before Christ, King Hammurabi of Babylon carved nearly three hundred laws into stone, many with prices in silver.

29
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:16,140
According to one law, a hired labourer earned about one shekel of silver a month.

30
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:24,640
A doctor who performed a successful operation could charge ten shekels, so silver was already setting prices almost four thousand years ago.

31
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:32,440
In Egypt, gold came fairly easily from the mines of Nubia, but silver had to be brought in from far away.

32
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:39,600
So for long stretches of Egyptian history, silver was reportedly valued even more highly than gold.

33
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:44,920
Egyptians said the gods had flesh of gold, and bones of silver.

34
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:54,820
In 1940, a French archaeologist named Pierre Montet opened the burial chamber of a pharaoh called Psusennes the First, at Tanis.

35
00:03:55,420 --> 00:04:00,700
Inside, the king lay in a coffin made of silver, with a gold mask on his face.

36
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:07,480
But the world was at war, and one of the greatest royal burials ever found barely made the news.

37
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:13,940
South of Athens, in a hilly area called Laurion, the Greeks had been mining silver for centuries.

38
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:20,420
Then, around 483 before Christ, miners hit an enormously rich new vein.

39
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:25,380
Most Athenians wanted to do the obvious thing, and share the money out among the citizens.

40
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:29,340
But a politician named Themistocles had another idea.

41
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:37,720
He persuaded the city to spend the silver on warships, building a fleet that grew to around two hundred fast triremes.

42
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:45,200
Officially, they were for a war with a nearby island, but Themistocles was worried about a much bigger enemy, Persia.

43
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:54,880
In 480 before Christ, the huge Persian fleet was lured into the narrow straits of Salamis, and the Greek ships crushed it.

44
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:01,320
Athens went on to its golden age, stamping its famous owl coins from the same mines.

45
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:08,780
But those mines were worked by enslaved people, by some estimates tens of thousands of them, deep underground.

46
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:13,740
Rome took the silver mines of Spain from Carthage, and worked them on a massive scale.

47
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:20,460
The Greek historian Polybius wrote that the mines near New Carthage alone employed forty thousand workers.

48
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:24,900
All that silver became the denarius, the coin that paid Rome's soldiers.

49
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:30,920
Roman smelting was so huge that its pollution travelled all the way to Greenland.

50
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:39,020
In 2018, scientists studying Greenland's ice found layers of lead pollution that rose and fell with the Roman economy.

51
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:44,280
Two thousand years later, the ice still remembers how much silver Rome made.

52
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:46,840
But then the emperors found a shortcut.

53
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:59,500
Starting around the time of Nero, they mixed more and more cheap metal into the coins, and by the late third century, a so-called silver coin was little more than copper with a thin silver coating.

54
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:05,960
Prices soared, and people stopped trusting the money of the most powerful empire on Earth.

55
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:12,940
Fast forward to 1516, in a valley called Joachimsthal, in what's now the Czech Republic.

56
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:20,240
Miners found rich silver there, and a local count began minting big silver coins called Joachimsthalers.

57
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:24,400
The name was a mouthful, so people shortened it to thaler.

58
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:28,000
The Dutch said daler, and English speakers said dollar.

59
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:37,340
American colonists used that word for the Spanish silver coins they handled every day, and after independence, the United States made it the name of its own money.

60
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:42,820
So the most powerful currency on Earth is named after a silver mining valley in Europe.

61
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:51,280
Around the same time, in India, the ruler Sher Shah Suri issued a silver coin called the rupiya, in the 1540s.

62
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,360
Its name comes from an old Sanskrit word connected with silver.

63
00:06:56,220 --> 00:07:00,760
Today, more than one and a half billion people still use money called the rupee.

64
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:09,280
In 1545, high in the Andes, in what's now Bolivia, a local man named Diego Huallpa made a discovery.

65
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:16,460
According to a famous story, he lit a fire on a mountainside, and melted silver began to trickle out of the ground.

66
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:24,380
Historians doubt the legend, but the silver was real, and the mountain became known as Cerro Rico, the rich hill.

67
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:30,600
The city of Potosí grew at its foot, more than four thousand metres above sea level.

68
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:39,720
By around 1610, it was reported to have about one hundred and sixty thousand people, roughly as big as London or Paris at the time.

69
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:46,980
In Spanish, something precious became worth a Potosí, a phrase even Don Quixote uses.

70
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:50,180
But that wealth came at a terrible cost.

71
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:59,040
From the 1570s, Spain forced indigenous villages to send their men to the mines, under a labour system called the mita.

72
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:09,440
Miners faced collapses, freezing cold and toxic mercury used to refine the ore, and Cerro Rico became known as the mountain that eats men.

73
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:16,260
Historians still argue about how many died, but over more than two centuries it was many thousands.

74
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:23,860
Potosí's silver was stamped into a Spanish coin called the piece of eight, which travelled to every corner of the world.

75
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:30,800
It became the first truly global currency, and it was legal money in the United States until 1857.

76
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:32,880
Here's a strange twist.

77
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:46,980
Early explorers heard rumours of a mountain of silver deep inland, so they named a great river the River of Silver, and the land around it became Argentina, from argentum, the Latin word for silver.

78
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:53,140
But Argentina itself had little silver, and the mountain they dreamed of was most likely Potosí.

79
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:59,340
Here's the part most history books leave out, much of that American silver didn't stay in Europe.

80
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:10,460
In the 1580s, Ming China began requiring many taxes to be paid in silver, and a country of well over a hundred million people suddenly needed enormous amounts of it.

81
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:14,660
China had silk, porcelain and tea, but not enough silver.

82
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:24,440
So from the 1570s, Spanish galleons sailed from Mexico across the Pacific to Manila, in the Philippines, loaded with silver.

83
00:09:25,220 --> 00:09:30,480
There, it was traded for Chinese goods, and the route ran for about two and a half centuries.

84
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:37,460
Some historians estimate that a third or more of all the silver mined in the Americas ended up in China.

85
00:09:37,460 --> 00:09:44,560
In 1859, miners in the hills of Nevada kept cursing a heavy blue mud that clogged their gold equipment.

86
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:50,000
Then someone tested it, and it turned out to be incredibly rich silver ore.

87
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:54,820
It was the Comstock Lode, the first major silver discovery in the United States.

88
00:09:55,200 --> 00:10:02,140
It was named after Henry Comstock, who reportedly sold his share for about eleven thousand dollars, and died broke.

89
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:09,240
Nevada's silver is often said to have helped the Union in the Civil War, and Nevada became a state in 1864.

90
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:16,400
And in the boomtown of Virginia City, a young reporter started signing his articles with a new name, Mark Twain.

91
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:22,740
For centuries, many countries used both gold and silver as money, at a fixed ratio.

92
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:33,120
But as Britain and then Germany moved to gold, the United States passed a law in 1873 that quietly stopped the free coining of silver dollars.

93
00:10:33,820 --> 00:10:38,820
Silver miners and farmers were furious, and they called it the Crime of 1873.

94
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:44,360
By the 1890s, gold against silver had become the biggest fight in American politics.

95
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:54,760
In 1896, a young politician named William Jennings Bryan gave a famous speech, warning that mankind must not be crucified on a cross of gold.

96
00:10:55,160 --> 00:11:00,080
He won the nomination for president, but he lost the election, and silver lost the war.

97
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:07,400
In 1839, Louis Daguerre unveiled one of the first practical forms of photography, made on silver-coated copper plates.

98
00:11:07,860 --> 00:11:14,620
For the next century and a half, almost every photograph and film relied on silver compounds that darken when light hits them.

99
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:21,560
Every family photo, every Hollywood movie and every hospital X-ray was, in a way, made of silver.

100
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:28,460
By the late 1990s, photography was using roughly a third of all the silver bought in the world, by some estimates.

101
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:36,180
Kodak, the giant of film, became one of the biggest silver users on Earth, and it even recovered silver from its own waste.

102
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:42,660
Then digital cameras arrived, and in 2012, Kodak filed for bankruptcy.

103
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:46,920
Meanwhile, silver was disappearing from people's pockets.

104
00:11:47,540 --> 00:12:00,680
As its price rose, the metal in coins became worth more than the coins themselves, so Britain dropped silver from its coins in 1947, and America from its dimes and quarters in 1965.

105
00:12:01,820 --> 00:12:05,320
In much of the world, everyday money stopped being silver.

106
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:11,580
Then came two brothers from a rich Texas oil family, Nelson Bunker Hunt and William Herbert Hunt.

107
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:20,080
In the 1970s, worried about inflation, they started buying silver, and then they kept buying, much of it with borrowed money.

108
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:24,940
By some estimates, they and their partners controlled well over a hundred million ounces.

109
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:29,960
In early 1979, silver cost about six dollars an ounce.

110
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:33,640
By January 1980, it had hit roughly fifty dollars.

111
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:37,960
Remember that number, fifty dollars an ounce, because it comes back later.

112
00:12:38,460 --> 00:12:44,300
Then the exchanges changed the rules, limiting new buying with borrowed money, and interest rates jumped.

113
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:54,740
On March twenty-seventh, 1980, now known as Silver Thursday, the Hunts couldn't meet a margin call, and silver fell to about ten dollars and eighty cents.

114
00:12:55,200 --> 00:13:01,260
The brothers lost an estimated one point seven billion dollars, and years later they declared bankruptcy.

115
00:13:01,860 --> 00:13:06,100
Today, silver isn't mostly money anymore, it's a working metal.

116
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:15,980
It's in phones, cars, wound dressings and data centres, and solar panels alone used nearly one hundred and ninety million ounces of it in 2025.

117
00:13:17,060 --> 00:13:22,520
For five years in a row, the world has used more silver than mines and recycling could supply.

118
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:24,580
And remember that fifty dollars.

119
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:32,480
For forty-five years it was the ceiling, until October 2025, when silver finally broke through it.

120
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:39,460
Then it went wild, and in late January 2026, it hit a record of about one hundred and twenty-one dollars an ounce.

121
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:45,740
The very next day, silver crashed by more than a quarter, one of its biggest one-day falls in history.

122
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:51,360
By this October, it was trading near sixty dollars, around half its peak.

123
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:58,840
And those high prices pushed solar factories to use less silver per panel, and to swap some of it for copper.

124
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:01,200
And remember what we said at the start.

125
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:15,800
By one industry estimate, around half of all the silver ever mined has already been used up or lost, scattered in tiny amounts across old electronics, landfills and rubbish dumps.

126
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:21,800
Gold gets locked in vaults and kept forever, but silver gets used.

127
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:24,120
So that's silver.

128
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:39,120
A metal forged in exploding stars, weighed out in Babylon, turned into warships at Athens, dug from a mountain that ate men, shipped to China, and bet away by two Texas brothers.

129
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:47,860
For most of history it was the money of ordinary people, and today it's the metal that quietly powers the modern world.

130
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:52,480
This is history, not financial advice, and nobody knows where the price goes next.

131
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:56,240
So what do you think will happen to silver in the next fifty years?

132
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,100
Tell me in the comments, and subscribe for the next long story.

